      "message": "I don\u0027t remember where I saw this rule, but when I am cleaning up a class, I am ordering the variable depending on the scope and visibility.\n\nFor example for test (same for others classes but without the rules and mocks)\n\n```\n@Rule\n\n@Mock\n@Spy\n\npublic final static VARIABLE\nfinal static VARIABLE\nprivate final static VARIABLE\n\npublic static sVariable\nstatic sVariable\nprivate static sVariable\n\n// I stop with public, package private, private but I keep it below too\n\npublic final mVariable \u003d new mFoo\u003c\u003e();\nfinal mVariable \u003d  new mFoo\u003c\u003e();\nprivate final mVariable \u003d  new mFoo\u003c\u003e();\n\n// still final but now the one that are initialized in constructor and that cannot be initialized directly here\npublic final mVariable; \nfinal mVariable; \nprivate final mVariable; \n\npublic mVariable\nmVariable\nprivate mVariable\n```\n\nThen sometimes, there is the need to gave a bit of adjustement when it makes more sense, like when there is a anonymous declaration, it\u0027s sometimes very noisy to make it with all the same one",
